76
00:06:56,910 --> 00:07:11,748
So these are AI models that generate a video world that you could move around in and Tencent's Arc lab released one called World Crafter recently and this one starts from just a single image or a single line of text.

77
00:07:12,174 --> 00:07:21,440
Now, one of the biggest issues that we've had with world models is that if you're inside a world model and you look away from something and then turn back, a lot of times it's like gone or changed.

78
00:07:21,774 --> 00:07:29,156
The model only remembers the last few seconds of what it showed you, and if you move around the room, everything kind of goes wonky.

79
00:07:29,358 --> 00:07:33,420
Now, world crafters fix is a memory that you look up by camera position.

80
00:07:33,838 --> 00:07:40,740
So, when you point the camera somewhere, it pulls the past views that match where you're looking and feeds those back to the generator.

81
00:07:40,740 --> 00:07:49,160
So, when you turn back to the original restaurant, it remembers what it saw the first time, so They prove it with this little trick here.

82
00:07:49,518 --> 00:07:54,800
They take the video the model generated and rebuild a 3D point cloud from it.

83
00:07:55,086 --> 00:08:00,660
If the world were inconsistent, that 3D reconstruction would come out as just like mush instead.

84
00:08:00,660 --> 00:08:06,100
You get clean geometry and the camera path lines back up when it revisits a spot.

85
00:08:06,382 --> 00:08:24,380
It handles moving things to like a character going out of frame and then later actually coming back into the frame, like we can see with this horse video here, where the camera takes the horse out of frame and then when the camera pivots back around to the right, you can see that the horse is still there, even though it was moving.

86
00:08:24,740 --> 00:08:33,940
Now on their own benchmark of 145 different scenes, they say that it beats the other models they tested on revisit consistency and camera control.

87
00:08:34,280 --> 00:08:37,160
