113
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:05,680
All right, so we already saw two world models trying to fix the memory problem.

114
00:11:06,020 --> 00:11:11,020
Well, Skyworks Matrix Game 4.0 takes a completely different approach.

115
00:11:11,020 --> 00:11:14,520
This one stops asking the AI to remember the world at all.

116
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:17,520
With a normal world model, the AI is doing everything.

117
00:11:18,040 --> 00:11:22,940
It's drawing the frame, but it's also the physics engine, the game rules, and the memory.

118
00:11:23,342 --> 00:11:28,840
When something leaves the screen, the only record it ever existed is in the pixels the model already drew.

119
00:11:29,140 --> 00:11:31,160
And that's kind of why things tend to vanish.

120
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:33,700
Matrix Games splits that job into two.

121
00:11:34,020 --> 00:11:36,140
An ordinary game engine runs the game.

122
00:11:36,560 --> 00:11:41,840
It knows where every enemy is, what happens when you shoot something, what's behind you.

123
00:11:42,040 --> 00:11:46,200
It draws a cheap, blocky, low poly version of that scene.

124
00:11:46,606 --> 00:11:51,920
Then the video model's only job is to repaint that blocky frame so it looks finished.

125
00:11:52,238 --> 00:11:57,600
So we can see in some of these models down here, on the right is what the game engine actually draws.

126
00:11:58,100 --> 00:11:59,960
On the left is what you see.

127
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:09,028
It's the same frame in the same moment, but the engine handles the gameplay and the AI handles the look that's skinned over the gameplay.

128
00:12:09,582 --> 00:12:20,840
Since this game engine is keeping track of everything, the world's not going to forget a pyramid behind you is still in the engines map, whether the AI is drawing it back in or not.

129
00:12:21,040 --> 00:12:22,860
Here's another example of a car game.

130
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:29,780
You can see the blocky early rendered version and then AI putting its own graphics over the top.

131
00:12:29,902 --> 00:12:33,980
So the game engine only has to remember this and the AI does that.

132
00:12:34,222 --> 00:12:35,268
There's also another piece.

133
00:12:35,406 --> 00:12:49,160
If you ask for something the game rules don't cover, a coding agent writes new rules into the engine and updates the prompt the video model uses, so the world can change mid-game without the AI having to invent new physics on the fly.

134
00:12:49,320 --> 00:12:55,080
Down here we can see three games, a medieval castle shooter, an ancient Egypt shooter, and city racing.

135
00:12:55,534 --> 00:13:00,132
These long play sessions can run up to about a minute and a half without any sort of cuts.

136
00:13:00,494 --> 00:13:01,980
Now, there are some catches.

137
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:09,940
Each game has its own model and each one is about 5 billion parameters each trained just for that scene.

138
00:13:10,126 --> 00:13:16,260
So it's not really a general make me any game model and the output is 480 by 864.

139
00:13:16,700 --> 00:13:18,840
So it's not anywhere near HD.

140
00:13:19,240 --> 00:13:31,600
Now the project page does talk about running on a single RTX 5090, but the model card does say there's no guaranteed frame rate, consumer GPU memory fit or exact scene reconstruction.

141
00:13:32,110 --> 00:13:41,360
So it may not totally fit into your GPUs memory and fast camera moves and long sessions still can cause some drift and the actual training code isn't included.

142
00:13:41,742 --> 00:13:48,120
But you can get the weights and the inference code under Apache 2.0 license so you can use them commercially.

143
00:13:48,540 --> 00:13:56,280
Now there is a hugging face space listed here for hugging apps matrix game 4.0 so you should be able to play around with it here.

