155
00:14:45,806 --> 00:14:49,540
That means road or grass or barrier or rival car.

156
00:14:49,660 --> 00:14:53,760
There's no lighting no textures, the video model actually draws everything you see.

157
00:14:54,094 --> 00:15:02,724
So here's the game engine's own view, the color map that it actually sends, and then the final frame that the user sees.

158
00:15:03,100 --> 00:15:09,600
Now, because the map carries no looks at all, you can actually restyle the whole game by swapping one reference image.

159
00:15:09,806 --> 00:15:21,620
We can see here that this is all the exact same drive, but when we fast forward a little bit, they can swap out the scene for anywhere they want and put the exact same drive in any different scene.

160
00:15:21,800 --> 00:15:29,240
It's the same exact input where we see in the middle, just rendered out in different locations and different settings during the day and things like that.

161
00:15:29,614 --> 00:15:37,980
So one thing to notice here, this is kind of one of the hard parts for this model is that the color map paints all of the rival cars, the same exact color.

162
00:15:38,254 --> 00:15:46,880
So the model has to actually remember on its own, which car is which and make sure to keep the same paint job across the various cars.

163
00:15:47,118 --> 00:15:53,580
So, a fun fact about this model is they actually handed cloud code the job of building a faster video decoder for this.

164
00:15:53,678 --> 00:15:58,860
They gave it a speed target, and then they had a working version running in the pipeline in about three hours.

165
00:15:59,118 --> 00:16:02,360
Now, this does actually require some serious hardware;

166
00:16:02,360 --> 00:16:06,440
each rendering step takes about 218 milliseconds.

